0:00:01.199,0:00:06.240
Building the mold is a relatively
simple stage, but it is important to

0:00:06.240,0:00:10.920
carry it out as correctly as possible,
because any mistake at this stage may lead

0:00:10.920,0:00:15.320
to resin leaks and problems that can be
difficult to fix

0:00:15.320,0:00:19.520
after the pour.
To build the mold, we use

0:00:19.520,0:00:24.439
inexpensive and easy-to-find materials. In
this case, I used a few leftover pieces

0:00:24.439,0:00:29.720
of melamine board to build
the frame required for the project. The first step

0:00:29.720,0:00:34.640
was drilling pilot holes in the areas
where the side walls will be fixed.

0:00:34.640,0:00:38.879
To make demolding easier after
the resin has cured, I applied adhesive tape

0:00:38.879,0:00:43.039
to all surfaces that will come into
contact with the resin.

0:00:43.039,0:00:47.320
After that, I positioned the frame on
the mold base and traced its outline

0:00:47.320,0:00:51.399
with a pencil. This allows us to know
exactly where

0:00:51.399,0:00:55.440
the side walls will be placed
and where the sealant must be applied.

0:00:55.440,0:00:59.600
Along the traced outline, I drilled all
the holes required for fixing the frame with

0:00:59.600,0:01:04.680
screws, allowing the assembly
to be completed quickly and accurately.

0:01:04.680,0:01:09.040
The next step is covering the entire
surface of the mold base with adhesive

0:01:09.040,0:01:14.040
tape. It is important that it is
stretched and applied properly, without

0:01:14.040,0:01:19.080
wrinkles or loose areas, in order to obtain
a surface that is as uniform as possible.

0:01:19.080,0:01:23.040
Before the final assembly of the frame,
we apply a continuous bead of silicone along

0:01:23.040,0:01:27.600
the previously marked outline. Its purpose
is to create a sealing gasket

0:01:27.600,0:01:31.280
between the mold base and the side walls and
to prevent resin leaks during

0:01:31.280,0:01:36.320
the pour. Once the frame has been positioned,
the screws are tightened

0:01:36.320,0:01:40.320
progressively, and the pressure created
compresses the silicone and forms the sealing layer

0:01:40.320,0:01:44.759
between the components. If some of the
silicone is pushed toward

0:01:44.759,0:01:49.520
the inside of the mold, it can be
removed or smoothed before

0:01:49.520,0:01:54.000
it cures. For additional protection,
we also apply silicone along the

0:01:54.000,0:01:58.000
outside joints, paying particular attention
to corners and

0:01:58.000,0:02:03.039
areas where leaks could occur.
After the silicone has cured and we are confident that

0:02:03.039,0:02:07.039
the entire assembly is stable,
we can move on to positioning

0:02:07.039,0:02:10.280
the wooden elements inside the
mold.

0:02:10.280,0:02:14.239
Depending on the project, we may choose to
seal larger pieces of wood by

0:02:14.239,0:02:18.519
applying a perimeter bead of
silicone. This method reduces

0:02:18.519,0:02:22.640
the amount of resin that flows beneath
the wood and can help reduce

0:02:22.640,0:02:26.560
material consumption.
In this particular project, I did not use

0:02:26.560,0:02:30.360
this approach. The wood contains
numerous cavities and voids, and

0:02:30.360,0:02:34.120
my goal is for the resin to penetrate
uniformly, including beneath the wooden

0:02:34.120,0:02:40.200
pieces. In this way, the first sealing coat
covers the entire surface as evenly as possible

0:02:40.200,0:02:45.239
and contributes to achieving
a final result that is more uniform and

0:02:45.239,0:02:47.640
more reliable.
